window.docContents[143].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Commutative-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___2___-Algebras--Commutative-algebra":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.2. Algebras","header":"Commutative algebra","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Commutative-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___2___-Algebras--Commutative-algebra"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Indecomposable-representation/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Indecomposable-representation--Indecomposable-representation--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"The two auxiliary predicates on a module are logically equivalent.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.3.8; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Indecomposable representation\u0009Indecomposable representation\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Indecomposable-representation/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Indecomposable-representation--Indecomposable-representation--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem--Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.8. The Krull-Schmidt theorem","header":"Scalar extension and the Noether-Deuring theorem","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem--Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___9___-Problems/The-Clifford-algebra/#Chapter3___Problem3___9___5":{"contents":"Problem 3.9.5. The Clifford algebra. Let V be a finite dimensional complex vector space equipped with a symmetric bilinear form ( \\ , \\ ). The Clifford algebra \\operatorname{Cl}(V) is the quotient of the tensor algebra TV by the ideal generated by the elements v \\otimes v - (v, v)1, v \\in V. More explicitly, if x_i, 1 \\leq i \\leq N, is a basis of V and (x_i, x_j) = a_{ij} then \\operatorname{Cl}(V) is generated by x_i with defining relations\n\nx_i x_j + x_j x_i = 2a_{ij}, \\quad x_i^2 = a_{ii}.\n\nThus, if ( \\ , \\ ) = 0, \\operatorname{Cl}(V) = \\wedge V.\n\n(i) Show that if ( \\ , \\ ) is nondegenerate, then \\operatorname{Cl}(V) is semisimple and has one irreducible representation of dimension 2^n if \\dim V = 2n (so in this case \\operatorname{Cl}(V) is a matrix algebra) and two such representations if \\dim(V) = 2n + 1 (i.e., in this case \\operatorname{Cl}(V) is a direct sum of two matrix algebras).\n\nHint: In the even case, pick a basis a_1, \\ldots, a_n, b_1, \\ldots, b_n of V in which (a_i, a_j) = (b_i, b_j) = 0, (a_i, b_j) = \\delta_{ij}/2, and construct a representation of \\operatorname{Cl}(V) on S := \\wedge(a_1, \\ldots, a_n) in which b_i acts as \"differentiation\" with respect to a_i. Show that S is irreducible. In the odd case the situation is similar, except there should be an additional basis vector c such that (c, a_i) = (c, b_i) = 0, (c, c) = 1 and the action of c on S may be defined either by (-1)^{\\text{degree}} or by (-1)^{\\text{degree}+1}, giving two representations S_+, S_- (why are they nonisomorphic?).\nShow that there is no other irreducible representations by finding a spanning set of \\operatorname{Cl}(V) with 2^{\\dim V} elements.\n\n(ii) Show that \\operatorname{Cl}(V) is semisimple if and only if ( \\ , \\ ) is nondegenerate. If ( \\ , \\ ) is degenerate, what is \\operatorname{Cl}(V)/\\operatorname{Rad}(\\operatorname{Cl}(V))?\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.9. Problems\u0009The Clifford algebra","header":"The Clifford algebra","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___9___-Problems/The-Clifford-algebra/#Chapter3___Problem3___9___5"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___1___-Maschke___s-theorem/Irreducible-representations-of-Z___pZ-in-characteristic-p-are-trivial/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___1___-Maschke___s-theorem--Irreducible-representations-of-Z___pZ-in-characteristic-p-are-trivial":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.1. Maschke's theorem","header":"Irreducible representations of Z/pZ in characteristic p are trivial","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___1___-Maschke___s-theorem/Irreducible-representations-of-Z___pZ-in-characteristic-p-are-trivial/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___1___-Maschke___s-theorem--Irreducible-representations-of-Z___pZ-in-characteristic-p-are-trivial"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___19___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Image-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_-in-End_LPAR_V_________n____RPAR_-spans-B/#Chapter5___Proposition5___19___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.19. Schur-Weyl duality for $GL(V)$\u0009Image of GL(V) in End(V^{⊗n}) spans B\u0009Image of GL(V) in End(V^{⊗n}) spans B","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___19___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Image-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_-in-End_LPAR_V_________n____RPAR_-spans-B/#Chapter5___Proposition5___19___1___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___19___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Schur-Weyl-decomposition-as-S_n-x-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_-representation/#Chapter5___Corollary5___19___2":{"contents":"Corollary 5.19.2. As a representation of S_n \\times GL(V), V^{\\otimes n} decomposes as \\bigoplus_\\lambda V_\\lambda \\otimes L_\\lambda, where L_\\lambda = \\operatorname{Hom}_{S_n}(V_\\lambda, V^{\\otimes n}) are distinct irreducible representations of GL(V) or zero.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.19. Schur-Weyl duality for $GL(V)$\u0009Schur-Weyl decomposition as S_n x GL(V) representation","header":"Schur-Weyl decomposition as S_n x GL(V) representation","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___19___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Schur-Weyl-decomposition-as-S_n-x-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_-representation/#Chapter5___Corollary5___19___2"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Transitivity-of-induction___-Ind_H___G-Ind_K___H-V-___-Ind_K___G-V/#Chapter5___Problem5___8___4___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.8. Induced representations\u0009Transitivity of induction: Ind_H^G Ind_K^H V ≅ Ind_K^G V\u0009Transitivity of induction: Ind_H^G Ind_K^H V ≅ Ind_K^G V","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Transitivity-of-induction___-Ind_H___G-Ind_K___H-V-___-Ind_K___G-V/#Chapter5___Problem5___8___4___formalization"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Corollary-6___8___4___-Existence-of-indecomposable-for-every-positive-root/#Chapter6___Corollary6___8___4":{"contents":"Corollary 6.8.4. For every positive root \\alpha, there is an indecomposable representation V with\n\n\nd(V) = \\alpha.\n\n\nProof. Consider the sequence\n\n\ns_n \\alpha, s_{n-1} s_n \\alpha, \\ldots.\n\n\nConsider the first element of this sequence which is a negative root (this has to happen by Lemma 6.7.2) and look at one step before that, calling this element \\beta. So \\beta is a positive root and s_i \\beta is a negative root for some i. But since the s_i only change one coordinate, we get\n\n\n\\beta = \\alpha_i\n\n\nand\n\n\n(s_q \\ldots s_{n-1} s_n) \\alpha = \\alpha_i.\n\nLet Q' be the quiver Q with orientation modified by the element s_q \\ldots s_{n-1} s_n. We let k_{(i)} be the representation of Q' having dimension vector \\alpha_i. Then we define\n\n\nV = F_n^- F_{n-1}^- \\ldots F_q^- k_{(i)}.\n\n\nThis is an indecomposable representation of Q and\n\n\nd(V) = \\alpha.\n\n\n\\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.8. Proof of Gabriel's theorem\u0009Corollary 6.8.4: Existence of indecomposable for every positive root","header":"Corollary 6.8.4: Existence of indecomposable for every positive root","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Corollary-6___8___4___-Existence-of-indecomposable-for-every-positive-root/#Chapter6___Corollary6___8___4"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Discussion-after-Example-8___1___7-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules--Discussion-after-Example-8___1___7-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.1. Projective and injective modules","header":"Discussion after Example 8.1.7 — Projective and injective objects in abelian categories","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Discussion-after-Example-8___1___7-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules--Discussion-after-Example-8___1___7-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories"}});